{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Mass.OneRowBlockLetterProduct","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Mass.OneRowBlockLetterProduct","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Algebra.Hom","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.BigOperators.Group.List.Basic"],"declarations":5,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.OneRowBlockLetterProduct.map_word_append","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.OneRowBlockLetterProduct.map_word_append","kind":"theorem","line":90,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/OneRowBlockLetterProduct.lean#L90-L99","doc":"**Factorisation over a concatenation.** The atom word is the free word with\ninterface letters inserted; under a one-dimensional block the character\nfactorises over any split of the letter list, so the interface letters\ncontribute a separate factor — one per bond, which is exactly \"bond additive\"\nonce a logarithm is taken on the archimedean side. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.OneRowBlockLetterProduct.map_word_eq_prod","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.OneRowBlockLetterProduct.map_word_eq_prod","kind":"theorem","line":78,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/OneRowBlockLetterProduct.lean#L78-L88","doc":"**The product formula.** Under a one-dimensional block, the character of a\nword is the product over its letters of `c·q + d`. This is the whole reason the\ndominant summand's log-weight cannot carry a connected many-body term at\n`A ≤ 4`: the word enters only through a product of per-letter scalars. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.OneRowBlockLetterProduct.map_letter","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.OneRowBlockLetterProduct.map_letter","kind":"theorem","line":73,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/OneRowBlockLetterProduct.lean#L73-L76","doc":"A one-dimensional block sends a letter to its scalar. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.OneRowBlockLetterProduct.letterValue","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.OneRowBlockLetterProduct.letterValue","kind":"def","line":68,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/OneRowBlockLetterProduct.lean#L68-L71","doc":"The scalar a one-dimensional block assigns to a letter when every generator\nis sent to `q`: `c·q + d`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.OneRowBlockLetterProduct.letter","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.OneRowBlockLetterProduct.letter","kind":"def","line":62,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/OneRowBlockLetterProduct.lean#L62-L66","doc":"An affine letter `c·σ_g + d·1` of the atom word: `mass_at_3A_proper.\natom_braid_word_3A` returns triples `(c, d, g)` denoting the Hecke element\n`c*sigma_g + d*1`. ","signatureChunk":0}]}